  quiz: {
    id: "qt-m5-quiz",
    title: "Module 5 Quiz",
    passPct: 70,
    questions: [
      { id: "q1", question: "Which is the best problem statement to start a 5 Whys?", options: ["The night shift made bad caps again", "Capper problem due to poor maintenance", "Line 2: 180 of 6 000 bottles checked on 12–14 May had cap removal torque below 1.0 N·m", "We need a new capper"], answer: 2, explain: "It gives what, where, when and how much, and contains no cause or solution." },
      { id: "q2", question: "When should a 5 Whys chain stop?", options: ["When it reaches a process or system cause that can be changed to stop recurrence", "After exactly five whys", "When a person responsible is identified", "When the team runs out of time"], answer: 0, explain: "Five is only a guide. Stopping at a person or a symptom leaves the real cause in place." },
      { id: "q3", question: "On a fishbone diagram, \"torque tester not calibrated for a year\" belongs on which bone?", options: ["Machine", "Method", "Material", "Measurement"], answer: 3, explain: "It concerns how the product is checked, so it is a measurement cause." },
      { id: "q4", question: "A PFMEA row has severity 8, occurrence 4 and detection 5. What is its RPN?", options: ["17", "160", "40", "1 000"], answer: 1, explain: "RPN = S × O × D = 8 × 4 × 5 = 160. Adding the ratings (17) or multiplying only two of them is a common mistake." },
      { id: "q5", question: "What is the purpose of D3 in an 8D?", options: ["Recognise the team", "Find the root cause", "Protect the customer with temporary containment until the permanent fix is in place", "Update the FMEA"], answer: 2, explain: "Containment is fast and temporary; it must be verified and later removed when the corrective action is validated." },
      { id: "q6", question: "In a control plan, what does the reaction plan column describe?", options: ["Who approved the control plan and when", "What to do with the process and the suspect product when a check fails or an SPC signal appears", "The tolerance of the characteristic", "The severity rating taken from the PFMEA"], answer: 1, explain: "The reaction plan tells the operator exactly what to do: stop or adjust the process, isolate the product made since the last good check, and alert the right people." },
      { id: "q7", question: "A sensor stops the press when a part is loaded upside down. What kind of poka-yoke is it?", options: ["Motion-step, warning", "Fixed-value, warning", "Fixed-value, control", "Contact, control"], answer: 3, explain: "It senses the position of the part (contact method) and stops the process (control function)." },
      { id: "q8", question: "How do you verify that a corrective action is effective?", options: ["Compare the defect rate over an agreed period after the action with the baseline before it", "Check that the action was done by the due date", "Ask the operator if things are better", "Close the CAPA when the customer stops complaining for one week"], answer: 0, explain: "Doing the action is not the same as proving it works. Data over time against a baseline shows effectiveness." },
    ],
  },
